    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>In the study of emotion and affective regulation, Richard Lazarus formulated the cognitive-mediational theory, which posits that cognitive appraisal serves as an indispensable and immediate intermediary between an environmental stimulus and the resulting physiological arousal and subjective emotional experience. Contradicting somatic-first theories such as the James-Lange model, Lazarus proposed that appraisal unfolds in two sequential phases: primary appraisal, wherein an individual categorizes an event as irrelevant, benign-positive, or stressful (subdivided into harm/loss, threat, or challenge); and secondary appraisal, wherein the individual assesses their available coping resources, cognitive options, and personal mastery to manage the demands.</p>\n<p>Concurrently, the facial feedback hypothesis, rooted in Darwinian evolutionary biology and popularized by Silvan Tomkins, asserts that proprioceptive and somatosensory afferent signals from facial efference directly influence subjective emotional experience and autonomic activation. Specifically, contracting the zygomaticus major muscle (active during smiling) enhances positive affect, whereas activating the corrugator supercilii muscle (active during frowning) intensifies negative emotional valence.</p>\n<p>To investigate how cognitive appraisal paradigms interact with facial muscular feedback, cognitive psychologists exposed 120 healthy undergraduates to an evaluative public speaking preparation task. Participants were randomly assigned to either a Threat appraisal condition (informed that speaking mistakes reveal underlying intellectual incompetence) or a Challenge appraisal condition (informed that the difficult exercise represents an opportunity to cultivate verbal communication skills). Within each appraisal condition, participants were instructed to hold a sanitized pen horizontally between their teeth without lips touching (activating the zygomaticus major while inhibiting lip closure) or held firmly between pursed lips (activating the orbicularis oris and inhibiting smile-related zygomatic contraction). Autonomic markers, facial electromyography (EMG), and state anxiety were quantified (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Autonomic arousal, facial electromyography, and state anxiety across experimental conditions</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Appraisal Induction</th><th>Facial Muscle Engagement</th><th>Mean Arterial Pressure (&Delta;MAP, mmHg)</th><th>Salivary &alpha;-Amylase (sAA, U/mL)</th><th>Corrugator EMG (&mu;V)</th><th>State Anxiety (STAI-S Score)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Threat Induction</td><td>Pursed Lips (Orbicularis Oris)</td><td>+18.4 &plusmn; 2.1</td><td>88.6 &plusmn; 7.4</td><td>15.2 &plusmn; 1.6</td><td>64.5 &plusmn; 4.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Threat Induction</td><td>Teeth Held (Zygomaticus Major)</td><td>+12.6 &plusmn; 1.5</td><td>66.2 &plusmn; 5.8</td><td>9.4 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>52.8 &plusmn; 3.7</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Challenge Induction</td><td>Pursed Lips (Orbicularis Oris)</td><td>+9.2 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>53.8 &plusmn; 4.9</td><td>7.6 &plusmn; 0.9</td><td>44.2 &plusmn; 3.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Challenge Induction</td><td>Teeth Held (Zygomaticus Major)</td><td>+3.8 &plusmn; 0.7</td><td>36.5 &plusmn; 3.4</td><td>3.5 &plusmn; 0.5</td><td>33.4 &plusmn; 2.6</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
