    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>Affective neuroscience delineates specialized subcortical limbic circuits and reciprocal prefrontal cortical networks that coordinate emotional reactivity and conscious emotional regulation. The amygdala functions as a rapid detector of emotional salience and environmental threat. Its basolateral complex (BLA) integrates polymodal sensory afferents from thalamic and cortical sensory areas to evaluate threat valence. The BLA subsequently projects to the central nucleus of the amygdala (CeA), which acts as the major affective motor output hub, projecting to the lateral hypothalamus (triggering sympathomedullary discharge) and the periaqueductal gray (orchestrating defensive freezing responses). Concurrently, the anterior insular cortex integrates visceral interoceptive feedback, representing subjective somatic feelings and processing disgust, while the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex (dACC) monitors affective conflict.</p>\n<p>Cortical emotion regulation is governed by distinct regions within the prefrontal cortex. The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (dlPFC) sustains executive control, working memory, and the deliberate generation of cognitive reappraisal strategies&mdash;an antecedent-focused technique that reframes the meaning of an emotional stimulus to modify its affective trajectory. In contrast, the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), which includes the medial orbitofrontal cortex, maintains dense anatomical projections to inhibitory GABAergic intercalated cell (ITC) masses within the amygdala, providing direct top-down suppression of central amygdalar output. Classical clinical case studies, such as that of Phineas Gage, illustrate that bilateral vmPFC damage produces profound emotional disinhibition, impaired moral judgment, and impulsive risk-taking despite preserved IQ, working memory, and semantic knowledge.</p>\n<p>To characterize the neural pathways mediating cognitive reappraisal, investigators conducted an event-related functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) study. Healthy adult controls ($n = 24$) and patients with acquired bilateral vmPFC lesions ($n = 12$) viewed neutral or high-arousal negative images under two instructional conditions: \"Passive View\" (maintain natural spontaneous emotional responses) and \"Reappraise\" (mentally reinterpret the image content to feel less negative). Blood-oxygen-level-dependent (BOLD) signal changes and subjective negative affect ratings were recorded (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Regional BOLD activation and negative valence ratings across experimental groups and conditions</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Participant Cohort</th><th>Condition &amp; Stimulus</th><th>dlPFC BOLD (% change)</th><th>vmPFC BOLD (% change)</th><th>Amygdala BOLD (% change)</th><th>Negative Affect Rating (1&ndash;9 Scale)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Healthy Controls</td><td>Passive View Neutral</td><td>+0.04 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>+0.03 &plusmn; 0.01</td><td>+0.05 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>1.6 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Healthy Controls</td><td>Passive View Negative</td><td>+0.14 &plusmn; 0.03</td><td>+0.08 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>+0.88 &plusmn; 0.07</td><td>7.5 &plusmn; 0.5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Healthy Controls</td><td>Reappraise Negative</td><td>+0.82 &plusmn; 0.06</td><td>+0.65 &plusmn; 0.05</td><td>+0.21 &plusmn; 0.03</td><td>3.4 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>vmPFC Lesion Patients</td><td>Passive View Neutral</td><td>+0.03 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>&minus;0.01 &plusmn; 0.01</td><td>+0.06 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>1.8 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>vmPFC Lesion Patients</td><td>Passive View Negative</td><td>+0.16 &plusmn; 0.04</td><td>&minus;0.02 &plusmn; 0.01</td><td>+0.92 &plusmn; 0.08</td><td>7.8 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>vmPFC Lesion Patients</td><td>Reappraise Negative</td><td>+0.79 &plusmn; 0.07</td><td>&minus;0.01 &plusmn; 0.01</td><td>+0.86 &plusmn; 0.07</td><td>7.3 &plusmn; 0.5</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
