    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>In his seminal work founding modern stress endocrinology, Hans Selye formulated the General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) to describe the organism's non-specific physiological response to sustained, noxious environmental challenges. Selye posited that regardless of the specific nature of the stressor&mdash;whether physical trauma, severe hypothermia, bacterial endotoxin, or chronic psychological restraint&mdash;the body mounts a stereotypical, triphasic physiological trajectory designed to maintain survival:</p>\n<p>1. <em>Alarm Reaction</em>: The immediate response to a sudden stressor, comprising an initial brief \"shock\" phase (marked by transient hypothermia, hypotension, and autonomic depression) followed rapidly by a \"counter-shock\" phase. During counter-shock, the sympathomedullary axis discharges catecholamines and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis initiates glucocorticoid secretion, mobilizing immediate physiological defenses.</p>\n<p>2. <em>Stage of Resistance (Adaptation)</em>: If the stressor endures, the organism enters a compensated state of active resistance. Adrenomedullary discharge subsides, but adrenocortical glucocorticoid secretion remains sustained. Glucocorticoids support hepatic gluconeogenesis and energy mobilization, allowing the organism to adapt to the specific ongoing challenge. However, this adapted state is metabolically costly, requiring the continuous suppression of anabolic processes, growth, tissue repair, and reproductive function.</p>\n<p>3. <em>Stage of Exhaustion</em>: Following prolonged, unremitting exposure, the organism's adaptive reserves are permanently depleted. Selye identified a hallmark pathological triad that characterizes this terminal stage: marked enlargement and lipid depletion of the adrenal cortex, profound thymolymphatic involution (atrophy of the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes), and acute hemorrhagic gastrointestinal ulcerations. Physiological regulation collapses, predisposing the organism to opportunistic infection, cardiovascular failure, and death.</p>\n<p>To characterize these morphometric and immunological dynamics across GAS stages, physiologists exposed adult Sprague-Dawley rats ($N = 60$) to continuous cold exposure (4 &deg;C) combined with daily restraint over 35 days. Cohorts were examined at Baseline (Day 0), Alarm (Day 1), Resistance (Day 14), and Exhaustion (Day 35) (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Morphometric, endocrine, and immunological parameters across the stages of General Adaptation Syndrome</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>GAS Phase &amp; Evaluation Day</th><th>Adrenal Wet Mass (mg / 100 g body)</th><th>Thymus Wet Mass (mg / 100 g body)</th><th>Plasma Corticosterone (ng/mL)</th><th>Splenocyte Proliferation (cpm &times; 10&sup3;)</th><th>Gastric Ulcer Severity (0&ndash;5 Scale)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Baseline Control (Day 0)</td><td>14.1 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>246 &plusmn; 15</td><td>44 &plusmn; 5</td><td>85.6 &plusmn; 6.4</td><td>0.0 &plusmn; 0.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Alarm Reaction (Day 1)</td><td>17.2 &plusmn; 1.4</td><td>214 &plusmn; 13</td><td>386 &plusmn; 26</td><td>64.2 &plusmn; 5.2</td><td>1.3 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stage of Resistance (Day 14)</td><td>22.8 &plusmn; 1.7</td><td>142 &plusmn; 10</td><td>228 &plusmn; 18</td><td>49.5 &plusmn; 4.6</td><td>0.5 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stage of Exhaustion (Day 35)</td><td>32.4 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>44 &plusmn; 5</td><td>416 &plusmn; 32</td><td>8.4 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>4.7 &plusmn; 0.5</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
