    "topic": "Taxonomy of Stressors: Cataclysmic, Major Life Events & Daily Hassles",
    "sheet": 1,
    "label": "Psychological, Social, and Biological Foundations of Behavior",
    "examId": "ps60_9",
    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Environmental and psychosocial stressors vary substantially across duration, population scale, predictability, and controllability. Behavioral epidemiologists and health psychologists classify stressors into four fundamental taxonomical categories:</p>\n<p>1. <em>Cataclysmic Stressors</em>: Sudden, devastating, large-scale events that impact entire communities or populations simultaneously (such as catastrophic earthquakes, tsunamis, volcanic eruptions, or wartime bombardments). Although unpredictable and severely traumatic, cataclysmic events frequently mobilize collective communal coping, shared grief, and reciprocal social support networks.</p>\n<p>2. <em>Major Life Events</em>: Significant personal transitions that disrupt an individual's established social roles or lifestyle (such as the death of a spouse, marital divorce, unexpected job loss, serious personal illness, or major financial bankruptcy). In 1967, Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe developed the Social Readjustment Rating Scale (SRRS) to quantify the cumulative impact of these events in standardized Life Change Units (LCUs). Holmes and Rahe hypothesized that any life change requiring substantial cognitive and behavioral readjustment&mdash;even subjectively positive transitions such as marriage, retirement, or outstanding personal achievement&mdash;acts as a physiological stressor.</p>\n<p>3. <em>Ambient Stressors</em>: Pervasive, chronic, low-level background environmental conditions that individuals cannot directly control (such as continuous urban traffic rumble, industrial air pollution, chronic workplace overcrowding, or extreme seasonal heat). Individuals frequently habituate consciously to ambient stressors while continuing to experience low-grade, persistent autonomic and allostatic strain.</p>\n<p>4. <em>Daily Hassles</em>: Minor, recurrent, irritating micro-events encountered in day-to-day life (such as misplacing household keys, navigating rush-hour traffic gridlock, experiencing brief disputes with coworkers, or managing tight routine deadlines). Longitudinal investigations by Richard Lazarus and Allen Kanner demonstrated that the cumulative frequency of daily hassles frequently predicts psychological distress and somatic health declines more strongly than major life events.</p>\n<p>A 24-month prospective epidemiological investigation followed 600 urban professionals. Participants were classified by baseline SRRS score (Low: &lt;150 LCUs; High: &ge;300 LCUs) and ongoing daily hassle frequency (Hassles and Uplifts Scale: Low vs High). Primary clinical outcomes included 24-month cumulative incidence of new-onset somatic disease (hypertension, ischemic heart disease, or ulcerative flares) and circulating high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) concentrations (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Somatic disease incidence, inflammatory biomarkers, and burnout scores across stressor cohorts</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Baseline SRRS Cohort</th><th>Daily Hassle Frequency</th><th>24-Month Disease Incidence (%)</th><th>Serum hs-CRP (mg/L)</th><th>Burnout Inventory Score (0&ndash;100 Scale)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Low LCUs (&lt;150)</td><td>Low Hassles</td><td>6.8%</td><td>1.1 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>22.4 &plusmn; 2.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Low LCUs (&lt;150)</td><td>High Hassles</td><td>19.2%</td><td>2.8 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>54.8 &plusmn; 4.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>High LCUs (&ge;300)</td><td>Low Hassles</td><td>16.4%</td><td>2.3 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>47.9 &plusmn; 3.8</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>High LCUs (&ge;300)</td><td>High Hassles</td><td>38.6%</td><td>4.9 &plusmn; 0.6</td><td>78.4 &plusmn; 5.2</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
