        "e": "<strong>Taxonomy of stressors: Operationalizing ambient stressors (Topic 887):</strong> <em>Ambient stressors</em> are chronic, pervasive, low-level background conditions embedded in the physical environment that cannot be readily altered or controlled by an individual (e.g., persistent toxic chemical odors, ambient noise pollution, urban smog, chronic overcrowding). Although individuals often consciously tune them out or habituate psychologically, ambient stressors continuously exert wear and tear on neuroendocrine and cardiovascular systems.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes an acute cataclysmic stressor (a sudden, catastrophic natural disaster impacting an entire community).<br>&bull; B describes major personal life events (divorce and home relocation, quantified as high LCUs on the Holmes-Rahe SRRS).<br>&bull; C describes a daily hassle (a minor, transient daily inconvenience and frustration)."
