        "e": "<strong>Sociological coping dynamics: Cataclysmic disasters versus personal crises (Topic 887):</strong> Cataclysmic stressors (e.g., tornadoes, floods, community-wide disasters) affect large populations simultaneously. Because the trauma is shared publicly across an entire community, it typically mobilizes collective solidarity, prosocial altruism, mutual aid, and communal grieving, which buffer psychological distress. In contrast, major personal life events (e.g., divorce, job termination, bankruptcy) are experienced individually and privately; they frequently carry social stigma, shame, and fractured interpersonal relationships, predisposing individuals to social isolation.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B directly inverts the social response patterns between cataclysmic and individual life events.<br>&bull; C is factually reversed; large-scale cataclysmic disasters trigger substantial institutional, governmental, and humanitarian relief.<br>&bull; D is absurd; cataclysmic disasters cause profound psychological trauma (e.g., acute stress disorder, PTSD)."
